    "finish_elapsed_s": 6.635801790980622,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making cold. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it passes through a compressor that squeezes it, making it hot. Then it travels through coils on the back, where the heat escapes into your kitchen. Next, the refrigerant expands and becomes very cold. It flows through pipes inside the fridge, where it absorbs heat from your food and air. Finally, it returns to the compressor to start again. So the fridge doesn't create cold—it grabs heat from inside and dumps it outside. That's why the back of a fridge feels warm!"
